            <ArticleTitle>The forgotten coil.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>291.e5-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.fertnstert.2010.06.075</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To raise awareness of forgotten intrauterine contraceptive devices (IUDs).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">District general hospital in London.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Two women, one with secondary infertility and one with heavy, painful periods, both unaware they had an IUD.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Removal of the forgotten IUD.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Return of fertility and improvement in gynecologic symptoms.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULT(S)" NlmCategory="RESULTS">After the removal of the IUD, the first patient who had had secondary infertility conceived within 1 month. In the second patient, the symptoms of menorrhagia and dysmenorrhea improved after removal of the IUD.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION(S)" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Although it is rare, a forgotten IUD is a potential cause of unexplained infertility and a differential diagnosis for gynecologic symptoms.</AbstractText>
